---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Signup from '../components/Signup.astro';

// Update this date every time the status on this page is re-checked.
const lastReviewed = 'October 16, 2026';
---
<Base
  title="Pennsylvania community solar status"
  description="Where Pennsylvania's community solar legislation (HB 504 / SB 504) stands, what it would do, and what happens next."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="eyebrow">Policy tracker</div>
      <h1>Where Pennsylvania stands on community solar</h1>
      <p><span class="status">Passed the House · Pending in the Senate</span></p>
      <p class="updated">Last reviewed: {lastReviewed}</p>

      <p>
        Pennsylvania doesn't have a statewide community solar program today. House Bill 504
        would create one. It passed the State House in May 2025 and now needs the State Senate
        to act.
      </p>

      <h2>Timeline</h2>
      <ul class="timeline">
        <li>
          <strong>2025: Bills introduced</strong>
          HB 504 (sponsored by Rep. Peter Schweyer) and the companion SB 504 (sponsored by
          Sen. Judy Schwank) are introduced in the 2025–2026 legislative session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>May 2025: Passed the State House</strong>
          HB 504 passed the Pennsylvania House with bipartisan support.
        </li>
        <li class="pending">
          <strong>Next: State Senate</strong>
          The bill needs to pass the Senate and be signed by the Governor. The 2025–2026 session
          ends November 30, 2026. Bills that haven't passed by then have to be reintroduced.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What the bill would do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Allow community energy projects</strong>, mainly solar, to be built and serve subscribers in Pennsylvania.</li>
        <li><strong>Let utility customers subscribe</strong> to a share of a local project and receive a credit on their electric bill.</li>
        <li><strong>Encourage participation by low- and moderate-income households</strong> and their communities.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep costs on participants</strong>, so customers who don't subscribe don't pay for the program.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What happens if it passes</h2>
      <p>
        If the bill becomes law, state regulators would still need to write the detailed program
        rules, such as how bill credits are calculated and how sign-up works. Projects would then
        need to be approved and built before subscriptions open. We'll explain each step here as
        it happens.
      </p>

      <div class="callout">
        Want to know when there's news? <a href="#notify">Join the update list</a>. We email only when something meaningful changes.
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://www.cpbj.com/community-solar-bill-passes-in-pa-house/" rel="noopener">Central Penn Business Journal: Community solar bill passes in Pa. House (May 2025)</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.pahouse.com/NorthEastDelegation/InTheNews/NewsRelease/?id=138514" rel="noopener">PA House Democrats: Schweyer bill for shared community energy clears PA House</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://www.palegis.us/legislation/bills/2025/sb504" rel="noopener">Pennsylvania General Assembly: SB 504 bill information</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <Signup />
</Base>
